WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.953
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.953 --> 00:00:02.587
Saturday the fourteenth.

00:00:02.587 --> 00:00:09.054
A cold, dry one, with a hard start, a slow breakfast, and a few hours of clay in the afternoon.

00:00:09.054 --> 00:00:11.572
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:11.572 --> 00:00:18.788
When you skip to minute three of a podcast, how does your phone avoid downloading the first three minutes?

00:00:18.788 --> 00:00:20.149
Answer's at the end.

00:00:20.149 --> 00:00:23.825
Weather's dry but cold, the chilliest day since March.

00:00:23.825 --> 00:00:32.266
Low forties this morning, upper forties at best, with a northwest wind around ten to fifteen that picks up in the afternoon.

00:00:32.266 --> 00:00:37.099
The forecast I found was from yesterday, so treat the numbers as rough.

00:00:37.099 --> 00:00:38.052
Okay, the day.

00:00:38.052 --> 00:00:41.116
Calendar has four by four intervals at seven.

00:00:41.116 --> 00:00:43.294
Then breakfast on there at nine.

00:00:43.294 --> 00:00:46.493
And a mug-making class from one-thirty to four.

00:00:46.493 --> 00:00:48.536
The evening's open after that.

00:00:48.536 --> 00:00:49.489
Alright, news.

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:50.510
Just two today.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:54.118
Flights across the whole country got grounded Friday.

00:00:54.118 --> 00:00:54.798
The F.A.A.

00:00:54.798 --> 00:01:01.265
called a nationwide ground stop after a communications failure hit air traffic control centers.

00:01:01.265 --> 00:01:02.422
Here's the twist.

00:01:02.422 --> 00:01:06.030
Federal officials are now checking whether a new A.I.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.004
routing system played a part.

00:01:08.004 --> 00:01:12.021
It's called SMART, and it started rolling out in September.

00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:16.650
It reroutes planes using live weather, congestion, and flight paths.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:23.389
Officials stress it's a telecom problem, there's no threat to anyone, and nobody has shown the A.I.

00:01:23.389 --> 00:01:24.069
caused it.

00:01:24.069 --> 00:01:27.405
But it's one of the first times a government A.I.

00:01:27.405 --> 00:01:31.081
system has landed in the middle of a failure this big.

00:01:31.081 --> 00:01:33.463
And one about a tool you might use.

00:01:33.463 --> 00:01:36.731
Notion says a problem at one of its outside A.I.

00:01:36.731 --> 00:01:40.407
vendors, a company called Fireworks, exposed some A.I.

00:01:40.407 --> 00:01:41.564
chat transcripts.

00:01:41.564 --> 00:01:45.376
Notion says it hit fewer than one percent of workspaces.

00:01:45.376 --> 00:01:51.366
It cut off the vendor, shut down the feature involved, and revoked any keys that leaked.

00:01:51.366 --> 00:01:53.341
The bigger lesson is quieter.

00:01:53.341 --> 00:01:59.263
When an app adds A.I., your stuff often passes through companies you've never heard of.

00:01:59.263 --> 00:02:01.782
Okay, here's one for later this week.

00:02:01.782 --> 00:02:07.159
Bushwick Gallery has an opening on Thursday the nineteenth, from four to eight.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:15.941
It's a solo show by an artist named Miles Forminard, called Introduction, and the gallery pitches it as all about line and space.

00:02:15.941 --> 00:02:18.459
The show runs through early December.

00:02:18.459 --> 00:02:24.654
They say visits are usually by appointment, so the opening is the easy way to just walk in.

00:02:24.654 --> 00:02:32.210
After an afternoon shaping a mug, it's a good week to go look at how someone else handles shape on a flat wall.

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.524
Today's challenge is a social one.

00:02:34.524 --> 00:02:38.949
At the class this afternoon, ask one person how they got into it.

00:02:38.949 --> 00:02:41.059
Not small talk, the real story.

00:02:41.059 --> 00:02:44.871
Then actually listen to the answer, all the way through.

00:02:44.871 --> 00:02:53.449
People who make things with their hands usually have a good one, and it's an easy way to leave a room knowing one more person.

00:02:53.449 --> 00:02:57.193
Okay, something new today, and it's right in your lane.

00:02:57.193 --> 00:03:01.753
How a camera sensor actually sees color, because it mostly doesn't.

00:03:01.753 --> 00:03:05.838
Each pixel on a sensor only measures how much light hits it.

00:03:05.838 --> 00:03:07.540
Brightness, nothing else.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.373
To get color, there's a tiny grid of colored filters laid over the top.

00:03:12.373 --> 00:03:15.232
Red, green, or blue, one filter per pixel.

00:03:15.232 --> 00:03:19.997
It's called a Bayer pattern, and it repeats in little squares of four.

00:03:19.997 --> 00:03:22.311
One red, one blue, and two greens.

00:03:22.311 --> 00:03:23.332
Why two greens?

00:03:23.332 --> 00:03:27.144
Your eyes pick up most of their detail from green light.

00:03:27.144 --> 00:03:29.935
So the sensor spends twice as much on it.

00:03:29.935 --> 00:03:33.407
That means every single pixel only knows one color.

00:03:33.407 --> 00:03:37.491
A red pixel has no idea how much green or blue landed on it.

00:03:37.491 --> 00:03:38.989
So the camera guesses.

00:03:38.989 --> 00:03:44.299
It looks at the neighbors and fills in the two missing colors for every pixel.

00:03:44.299 --> 00:03:46.477
That step is called demosaicing.

00:03:46.477 --> 00:03:51.174
Two-thirds of the color in every photo you take is an educated guess.

00:03:51.174 --> 00:03:53.012
Usually the guess is great.

00:03:53.012 --> 00:03:55.803
Where it breaks is fine repeating detail.

00:03:55.803 --> 00:03:59.070
A tight weave, a grille, finely engraved silver.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:07.579
The pattern gets close to the size of the filter grid, the guess gets confused, and you see rainbow shimmer that isn't there.

00:04:07.579 --> 00:04:08.464
That's moire.

00:04:08.464 --> 00:04:11.596
A raw file stores the mosaic before the guess.

00:04:11.596 --> 00:04:17.041
That's partly why two raw editors can render the same file a little differently.

00:04:17.041 --> 00:04:20.037
And here's the trick for still product work.

00:04:20.037 --> 00:04:22.555
Some cameras have a pixel shift mode.

00:04:22.555 --> 00:04:30.792
The sensor nudges itself one pixel at a time between shots, so every spot gets measured in red, green, and blue for real.

00:04:30.792 --> 00:04:35.966
No guessing, cleaner color, and the shimmer on fine detail mostly goes away.

00:04:35.966 --> 00:04:41.752
It only works when nothing moves, which a piece of silverware on a table is great at.

00:04:41.752 --> 00:04:47.810
So: calendar says intervals at seven, breakfast at nine, and the mug class at one-thirty.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.444
Ask someone their story.

00:04:49.444 --> 00:04:55.162
The answer: it asks the server for just that slice of bytes, called a range request.

00:04:55.162 --> 00:04:57.000
Have a good Saturday, Ziad.
